\section{Mailbox Arrival Is Not Token Matching}

Actors encapsulate state and react to messages, possibly creating actors, sending messages, and choosing behavior for later messages \cite{hewitt1973actor}. Dataflow actors also communicate, but a particular model may require FIFO channels, fixed token rates, firing only when a complete pattern is present, or deterministic process behavior. The words ``actor'' and ``message'' do not supply those guarantees by themselves.

This Erlang-style server is deliberately order-sensitive:

\begin{verbatim}
counter(N) ->
  receive
    {add, K}     -> counter(N + K);
    {read, From} -> From ! {value, N}, counter(N)
  end.
\end{verbatim}

Two senders racing to send \texttt{\{add,1\}} and \texttt{\{read,...\}} may cause the read to observe different states, subject to the language's delivery guarantees. A deterministic Kahn process network instead obtains a determinate stream function under its assumptions even though process execution is concurrent. CAL can express dynamic action selection and priorities; determinism must be proved for the actor or network, not inferred from graph shape.

\section{Choose a Communication Contract in the IR}

A concurrency IR must say whether a channel is FIFO globally, FIFO per sender, unordered, bounded, lossy, or blocking; whether receive uses patterns; and whether sends, state updates, and outputs are atomic together. Actor identity, creation, supervision, timeouts, failure, and external effects cannot be represented as ordinary pure data edges. A deterministic subdialect can carry a certificate or effect saying why schedule choice is unobservable.

